{"format": "long", "channel": "history", "style": "mapminutes", "voice": "bm_george", "speed": 1.1, "projection": "canada", "id": "mm-newfoundland", "title": "Why Newfoundland Joined Canada Last: The Split Vote", "summary": "Newfoundland stayed out of Canada in 1867 and voted against joining in 1869. Broke in 1934, it gave up responsible government; in 1948 two referendums ended 52.3% for Confederation, with the Avalon Peninsula around St. John's voting the other way. It became Canada's tenth province at 11:59 p.m. on 31 March 1949.", "tags": ["newfoundland", "newfoundland confederation", "1948 referendum", "joey smallwood", "newfoundland and labrador", "why did newfoundland join canada", "canadian history", "map minutes"], "intro_chapter": "The island that said no", "thumbnail": {"ax": 620, "ay": 420, "fw": 1000, "pin_x": 200, "pin_y": 705, "pin_r": 160, "words_top": 40, "words_right": 50, "words_fs": 130, "needle": 120, "lean": 6, "layers": {"parallel49": false}, "words": ["CAPITAL", "SAID NO"], "expr": "surprised", "cam": {"fit": [-61.5, 45.6, -49.0, 52.4], "pad": -0.02}, "callout": {"at": [-52.71, 47.56], "arrow_from": [1030, 330], "bend": 40}}, "sources": ["Heritage Newfoundland and Labrador, The 1948 Referendums (3 June 1948: responsible government 69,400 votes, 44.6%; Confederation 64,066, 41.1%; Commission 22,311, 14.3%; 22 July 1948: Confederation 78,323, 52.3%, responsible government 47.7%; Avalon 66% responsible government, other districts 70% Confederation; Economic Union Party led by Chesley Crosbie promoted a special economic relationship with the United States; Confederate Association launched 21 February 1948): https://www.heritage.nf.ca/articles/politics/referendums-1948.php", "The Canadian Encyclopedia, Newfoundland and Labrador and Confederation (1869 election: 'the anti-Confederation side won a decisive victory'; by the 1920s in debt, the Depression made it worse; 'Facing bankruptcy, Newfoundland's leaders suspended responsible government in 1934', an unelected Commission Government directed by Britain; 1946 elected National Convention; 52.34% in the second referendum; Smallwood elected first premier; name Newfoundland and Labrador from 2001): https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/newfoundland-and-labrador-and-confederation", "The Canadian Encyclopedia, Newfoundland Joins Canada (Smallwood: 'If you are not a millionaire, vote for Confederation'; St. John's voted 2-1 for self-government, outports pro-Canada; 'At 11:59 the previous night, Newfoundland had become a Canadian province'): https://thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/newfoundland-joins-canada-feature", "CBC, When Newfoundland became Canada's 10th province ('The Union came into effect just before midnight on March 31, 1949'): https://www.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.5045846", "Constitution Act, 1867 (Canada formed by the union of Ontario, Quebec, Nova Scotia and New Brunswick, 1 July 1867): https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/const/page-1.html", "82 years (1867 to 1949): our arithmetic."], "score": [{"scene": 0, "mood": "wonder"}, {"scene": 4, "mood": "explore"}, {"scene": 8, "mood": "tension"}, {"scene": 12, "mood": "explore"}, {"scene": 19, "mood": "payoff"}], "scenes": [{"type": "map", "narration": "When Newfoundland finally voted to join Canada, nearly half the island said no. And its capital voted the other way.", "cam": [{"fit": [-59.8, 46.4, -52.3, 51.9], "pad": 0.04, "t": 0}, {"fit": [-59.8, 46.4, -52.3, 51.9], "pad": -0.01, "t": "end", "ease": "sine.inOut"}], "data": {"layers": {"routes": [{"via": [[-53.542, 48.108], [-53.569, 48.088], [-53.704, 48.068], [-53.87, 48.02], [-53.794, 48.01], [-53.653, 48.026], [-53.638, 48.015], [-53.695, 47.921], [-53.864, 47.787], [-53.805, 47.682], [-53.765, 47.65], [-53.672, 47.648], [-53.604, 47.662], [-53.504, 47.744], [-53.283, 47.998], [-52.921, 48.147], [-52.866, 48.113], [-52.998, 47.976], [-53.154, 47.735], [-53.176, 47.653], [-53.17, 47.512], [-53.122, 47.455], [-52.945, 47.553], [-52.873, 47.